WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.000
I'm at the Eureka weather station in Nunavut,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.500
in the Canadian High Arctic, about 80 degrees
north.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.800
Here they provide weather reports not just
for the Arctic,

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.970
but for the transatlantic flights high above
it.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.350
Because we're in Nunavut, there are three
official languages here:

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.679
English, French, and the local language: Inuktitut.

00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:21.939
Although it’s difficult to be exact about
languages in the Arctic:

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:25.160
the line between dialects and languages gets
blurred,

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.429
people in different areas can understand some
of people say.

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:33.630
Like most languages here, Inuktitut is polysynthetic,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.590
which means that something that needs a whole
sentence in English

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.840
can just be one long word,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.030
with each part affecting all the others.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.770
Now, in Greenland, similar languages are written
using the Latin alphabet,

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.800
but the words can seem unwieldy to English-speakers.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.380
The Latin alphabet is a writing system that
evolved for a completely different language,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.420
one with lots of short words and spaces.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.850
But there is an alternative,

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.570
it's used all round northern Canada,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.930
and to my eyes, it’s really cool.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.620
A bit of history: the reason this writing
system exists is because

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.820
it was invented by Christian missionaries
who were trying to spread the gospel.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.609
The first books printed in Inuktitut were
extracts from the Bible.

00:01:11.609 --> 00:01:15.130
I’ve put some links in the description to
provide some context there, but:

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:20.500
there’s no way I can cover that in a short
video that’s mostly about linguistics.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.700
First up: this is not an alphabet.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.899
In an alphabet, vowels and consonants both
have letters,

00:01:26.899 --> 00:01:28.789
which makes sense when you’ve speaking a
language

00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:32.570
with a lot of complicated combinations of
sounds in it.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.920
But Inuktitut, like a lot of polysynthetic
languages,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.079
has a relatively simple syllable structure:
with rare exceptions,

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:41.369
the maximum you can have in a syllable is

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:45.240
a consonant, some vowels, and then another
consonant.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.369
That is it, that is all your options.

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:51.509
A long, English one-syllable word like “scratched”
wouldn’t work here:

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:54.139
you need to put vowels in those consonant
clusters.

00:01:54.139 --> 00:01:56.479
You need se-ce-ra-te-ched.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:01:59.360
Long vowels being written with two letters,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.619
that's also added to the long words,

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:04.539
so the Latin alphabet gets a bit overwhelmed.

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:06.659
But the Inuktitut script is not an alphabet.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.649
It is an abugida.

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:12.049
Each symbol represents both a consonant and
a vowel.

00:02:12.049 --> 00:02:15.090
The shape of the symbol tells you the consonant,
and here is the clever part:

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.920
the direction it's pointing tells you the
vowel.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.330
And if it’s got a dot above it,

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.650
it’s a long vowel, like aa instead of a.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.830
I’m not going to get the vowels exactly
right, I’m English.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.220
And if there’s a little symbol, up afterwards,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.400
you add another consonant at the end.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.390
So my name, Tom, would be like this:

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.040
the curve shape means T,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.299
it's pointing to the right, which means “u”,

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:35.790
the closest you can get to an o here,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.450
and the right angle at the end means a closing
M.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:39.450
Tum.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.530
If your accent's different,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:41.829
you might transcribe it a little differently,

00:02:41.829 --> 00:02:43.360
you might flip the curve the other way.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.360
Tam.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.360
And if you're American,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.849
you'd add a little dot there for a long vowel.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:48.870
Taam.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.849
So here is a simple word in Inuktitut: anaana.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:52.849
Mother.

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:54.379
Like almost every language in the world,

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:56.799
the word for mom is a sound that a baby will
make accidentally.

00:02:56.799 --> 00:02:59.340
Sorry parents, it was probably an accident.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.610
A triangle means no consonant,

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.349
and pointed left always means a simple “a”.

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:07.140
Next, that shape means “n”, pointing left
means “a”,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.420
and the dot means long.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.420
“naa”.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.180
Then the same again, without the dot. “na”.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.180
Annana.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.230
The word for father is ataata.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.239
Swap those spools out for the curve,

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:18.500
the T from Tom, and there you go.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.500
Ataata.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.200
And yes, of course it’s more complicated
than this,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.060
I’m literally speaking baby talk,

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:26.390
but compare this to the Latin alphabet

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:30.909
where there's just arbitrary symbols that
you have to learn.

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:32.430
There’s design here.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.920
There's intent.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.019
There isn't complicated letters with unwritten
rules that all influence each other.

00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:39.540
This is an easy script to learn,

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.769
and for an endangered language with only about
30,000 fluent speakers?

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:46.489
That is a really good attribute to have.

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:50.410
I'm here thanks to Chris Hadfield's Generator
Arctic, and so are these two!

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.439
-- I'm Norm from Tested.
-- And I'm Joey from Tested.

00:03:52.439 --> 00:03:54.510
-- And we're here shooting a bunch of educational
videos as well.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.480
You can find those at youtube.com/testedcom

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.430
Links are in the description or on screen
now!

